<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <%# Per-page titles: set with `content_for :title` in a view.
        WCAG 2.4.2 - each page needs a title describing its topic. %>
    <title><%= content_for?(:title) ? "#{yield(:title)} | Stocks in the Future" : "Stocks in the Future" %></title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
    <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
    <%= csp_meta_tag %>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag :app, 'data-turbo-track': "reload" %>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag "actiontext", 'data-turbo-track': "reload" %>
    <%= javascript_importmap_tags %>
  </head>
  <body class="min-h-screen bg-slate-50" data-controller="drawer">
    <a href="#main-content" class="sr-only focus:not-sr-only focus:absolute focus:top-2 focus:left-2 focus:z-100 focus:rounded-md focus:bg-sitf-primary focus:px-4 focus:py-2 focus:text-sitf-on-primary focus:outline-2 focus:outline-offset-2 focus:outline-sitf-primary-dark">Skip to main content</a>
    <% if user_signed_in? %>
      <%# Same treatment as the admin app bar: white against the slate-50 page, with the
          hairline bottom edge design.md blesses for fixed chrome. It was a full-width 1px bar
          in sitf-primary-dark (8.5:1, body-text weight for furniture), then briefly shadow-xs
          while the header was the same colour as the page. %>
      <%# px-4 lg:px-6 matches <main> below. As px-6 at every width the header's contents sat 24px in
          while the cards sat at 16px on a phone, so the chrome and the content did not share an edge. %>
      <%= render "layouts/environment_ribbon" %>

      <%# `min-h-16` and no vertical padding, and its height is measured.

          `h-16` with `py-4` was 64px of box around 76px of content, so at 200% text the contents spilled
          out of the border rather than moving it. Its admin twin had the mirror-image bug: `min-h-16`
          with `py-3` measured **69px**, which put the header's bottom border 5px over the top of the
          sidebar - an odd line across the nav, and the nav's own rule not continuing.

          The trigger is already 44px, so `min-h-16` alone gives exactly the 64px this has always been,
          and lets it grow only when the contents genuinely need it. `chrome-offset` publishes what it
          measures so the sidebar and the content follow. %>
      <header class="chrome-header fixed left-0 z-50 w-full border-b border-slate-200 bg-white"
              data-controller="chrome-offset"
              data-chrome-offset-variable-value="--sitf-header-h">
        <%# The row is a child of the bordered box, mirroring admin's structure, and that is not
            cosmetic. Preflight sets `box-sizing: border-box`, so `min-h-16` *on the bordered element*
            leaves 63px of content - the two layouts published 63 and 64 and their sidebars aligned with
            different numbers. With the border on the outer box and the height on the inner row, both
            measure the same 64px. %>
        <%# No `flex-wrap` here, unlike admin's row, and the difference is the content rather than
            carelessness. Admin's left side is a *text* wordmark that grows with the font, so at 200% it
            has to be allowed to wrap or it pushes the page sideways. This side's is a fixed-size SVG,
            and wrapping it at 375px moved the account menu onto a second line at the left edge - 272px
            off the gutter it shares with every card on the page, which `chrome_gutter_test` caught.
            Measured: `reflow_test` passes at 320px and at 200% text without it. %>
        <div class="flex min-h-16 items-center justify-between gap-2 px-4 lg:px-6">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4">
          <%# A button, not a label driving a hidden checkbox: a label is announced as a
              checkbox rather than as a control that opens navigation.

              No negative margin. The control's box is the thing that paints, so the box is what
              aligns to the gutter - flush with the cards below it at 16px. Two attempts at
              pulling it out to put the *glyph* on the gutter instead both failed, because
              whatever paints comes with it: first a filled button, then a hover fill, then a
              40px state layer, each hanging 6-8px past the content edge. The glyph sits 10px
              inside its own box because a 44px target centres a 24px icon; that is inherent, and
              it is the box the eye reads.

              rounded-lg, not rounded-full: design.md's radius token is `controls rounded-lg`.
              slate-600 on white is 7.58:1. %>
          <button type="button"
                  data-drawer-target="trigger"
                  data-action="drawer#toggle"
                  data-testid="open-navigation"
                  aria-expanded="false"
                  aria-controls="main-navigation"
                  class="lg:hidden flex min-h-11 min-w-11 items-center justify-center rounded-lg text-slate-600 transition-colors hover:bg-slate-100 hover:text-slate-900 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-sitf-primary">
            <span class="sr-only">Open navigation menu</span>
            <svg class="w-6 h-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h16" />
            </svg>
          </button>
          <%= image_tag "SITF-Horz-no-tag-logo.svg", class: "h-8 w-auto", alt: "Stocks in The Future" %>
        </div>
        <%# The scrolling stock ticker was here, 400px wide, `hidden lg:block`. Removed: see
            migration.md. It failed WCAG 2.2.2 at Level A - content moving automatically for more than
            five seconds with no way to pause, stop or hide it - its two colours measured 2.74:1 and
            3.78:1, and it showed a symbol and a percentage with no price, no company name and no link.
            Its content lives on the trading floor now, as a Change column beside Last price - which is
            where every brokerage list puts a price change, and beside the price it is actually about. It
            spent one commit as a card on the home page, where it pushed the balance, the announcements and
            the getting-started steps down to list three of the companies the trading floor lists anyway. %>
        <%# The badge and the account menu share a group, so the badge sits with the other chrome
            rather than floating between the logo and the avatar. %>
        <div class="flex shrink-0 items-center gap-2">
          <%= render "layouts/environment_badge" %>
          <%= render 'layouts/account_menu' %>
        </div>
        </div>
      </header>

      <%= render 'layouts/navbar' %>

      <%# p-4 lg:p-6 on all four sides, the same gutter the signed-out branch below uses and the
          same one admin's inner wrapper uses. This was `px-4 lg:px-6 ... pb-6`: horizontal padding
          and bottom padding, with **no padding-top**, so the page title sat hard against the bottom
          edge of the fixed 64px nav on every signed-in page. mt-16 clears that nav and is not
          padding, which is what made the gap easy to miss - and design.md asserted this element was
          `p-4 lg:p-6` while only the signed-out branch was. %>
      <main id="main-content" class="chrome-main-margin min-h-screen p-4 lg:p-6 ml-0 lg:ml-64 bg-slate-50">
        <%# The content column lives here, around the flash *and* the page, because the flash has to
            be the same width as the thing it sits above and only the layout can guarantee that.
            It used to be per-page: home, stock#show and portfolio wrapped themselves in
            `mx-auto max-w-7xl` while the flash was a bare child of <main>, so at 1920px the
            sign-in banner measured 1601px against cards of 1280px - 321px wider, and starting
            161px to their left. Below ~1584px nothing shows, because that is where main's content
            box stops exceeding max-w-7xl, which is why this survived. Constraining the flash alone
            would have inverted the bug on the four pages that span full width. %>
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl">
          <%= render 'layouts/flash' %>

          <%= yield %>
        </div>
      </main>
    <% else %>
      <%# Signed out there was no header at all, so the only route in was whatever page you
          happened to land on. The logo and one Sign in action, matching the signed-in bar. %>
      <%= render "layouts/environment_ribbon", fixed: false %>

      <header class="flex h-16 w-full items-center justify-between border-b border-slate-200 bg-white px-6 py-4">
        <%= link_to root_path, class: "inline-flex items-center rounded-sm focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-sitf-primary-dark" do %>
          <%= image_tag "SITF-Horz-no-tag-logo.svg", class: "h-8 w-auto", alt: "Stocks in The Future" %>
        <% end %>

        <div class="flex shrink-0 items-center gap-2">
          <%= render "layouts/environment_badge" %>

          <% unless request.path == new_user_session_path %>
            <%= link_to "Sign in", new_user_session_path, class: "tw-btn-primary" %>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      </header>

      <main id="main-content" class="min-h-screen p-4 lg:p-6 bg-slate-50">
        <%= render 'layouts/flash' %>

        <%= yield %>
      </main>
    <% end %>

    <%# Trading Modal %>
    <%= render 'shared/modal' %>

    <%# Replaces the native confirm() for every data-turbo-confirm in the app. Outside <main> because it
        is chrome rather than page content, and rendered for signed-out visitors too - cheap, and it means
        the layout does not have to know which pages carry a destructive action. %>
    <%= render 'shared/confirm_dialog' %>
  </body>
</html>
